topic|Bitcoin|https://www.coindesk.com/m.text:
{
  "stance": "bearish",
  "view": "Bitcoin is under pressure. It fell to about $82,300, roughly 4% below Tuesday's high near $86,600, and spot ETFs saw $487.1 million in net outflows on Wednesday. The 30-year Treasury yield rose to 5.71% and Fed minutes showed all 19 officials backed the last rate hike, with most seeing another by year-end. Derivatives show de-risking rather than fresh shorts, so the selling is less forced but sellers still hold the edge.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Bitcoin slides as ETF outflows and rising yields weigh",
      "essay": "Bitcoin dipped to about $82,300 during Asian hours before recovering to around $82,800, roughly 4% below Tuesday's high near $86,600. The CoinDesk 100 lost close to 2% over 24 hours.\n\nU.S. spot bitcoin ETFs recorded $487.1 million in net outflows on Wednesday, reversing $118.8 million in inflows the previous day. BlackRock's IBIT led with $207.7 million, followed by Fidelity's FBTC at $105.2 million and Ark Invest/21Shares' ARKB at $101.7 million. The funds have seen $165.6 million in net outflows over the past five trading sessions after a prior $3.1 billion nine-day inflow streak.\n\nBond markets added pressure. The 30-year Treasury yield climbed 4 basis points to 5.71% and the 10-year to 5.32%. Minutes of the Fed's September meeting showed all 19 officials backed last month's rate increase, with most seeing another as likely appropriate by year-end.\n\nDerivatives positioning shows sellers still in control but with less forced selling. The 24-hour taker long/short ratio is 48% to 52%. Crypto futures open interest is down 1% at $150 billion, and liquidations fell to $400 million from $548 million yesterday. Notional open interest has fallen in BTC, ETH, HYPE, XRP and DOGE by as much as or more than their spot prices, meaning traders are not adding new bearish bets. Bitcoin's one-week put-call skew rose to 10%, a bias for downside protection.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "headline": "US government moves $1.5 billion in seized bitcoin to Coinbase",
      "essay": "Wallets linked to the U.S. government moved 17,795 bitcoin worth $1.5 billion over the past three days, according to Arkham Intelligence and Galaxy Research. About 9,261 BTC, valued at around $770 million, went to Coinbase Prime on October 7-8. A separate transfer of 12,267 bitcoin, worth about $1.01 billion, left a wallet holding funds seized in the 2016 Bitfinex hack and went to a new, unlabeled address. No exchange deposit was recorded for that move, a pattern more consistent with wallet reshuffling than a sale.\n\nCoinbase Prime is the institutional arm of Coinbase and has served as a custody provider for the US Marshals Service since 2024. A March 2025 executive order directed that forfeited bitcoin be placed into a Strategic Bitcoin Reserve and said it should not be sold. The government still holds about $25.5 billion worth of crypto, Arkham shows, or an estimated 319,000 to 325,000 BTC.\n\nThe transfers sparked short-term speculation, but the stated policy points to custody management rather than liquidation. The victim compensation exception in the executive order is the detail to watch, since some seized coins have identifiable victims and returning funds could require moving or converting assets.",
      "sources": [3, 4, 5]
    },
    {
      "headline": "QCP sees $80,000-$90,000 Q4 range for bitcoin",
      "essay": "QCP Capital forecasts bitcoin will trade between $80,000 and $90,000 in its base case for the fourth quarter. The firm identified $80,000 to $82,000 as an area to buy and said it would reduce exposure around $88,000 to $90,000 if ETF inflows have not picked up. It expects ETF inflows to remain positive but inconsistent, with oil prices elevated but stable and uncertainty around the Clarity Act weighing on the market.\n\nQCP's bull case puts bitcoin above $100,000, supported by sustained ETF inflows, growth in stablecoin supply, a Fed pause on weaker employment data, a softer dollar, lower real yields, and progress on the Clarity Act. Its bear case sees bitcoin below $68,000 to $70,000 if Middle East fighting intensifies, oil prices rise, and the Fed hikes further, along with renewed ETF outflows or a corporate holder forced to sell.\n\nGlassnode said bitcoin fell back from a sell wall at $86,500 toward large buy orders, the biggest around $81,000, which it expects to cushion further declines. Kraken Chief Economist Thomas Perfumo said previous cycles took 12 to 13 months from high to bottom, but this cycle's 54% drawdown to its July low is smaller than the 70% to 85% typical declines. He said long-term interest rates remain one of the most important variables for bitcoin's next move.",
      "sources": [2]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Gold|https://www.kitco.com/news/ar.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Gold is caught between rising yields and safe-haven demand. Prices are near $4,120, up $99 from yesterday but down from January's record. Central bank buying continues, with China adding 23 tonnes in September. The tug-of-war leaves no clear direction today.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Gold rises $99 as yields hit 24-year high",
      "essay": "Gold climbed $99 to $4,117 per ounce as the 10-year Treasury yield touched 5.35%, its highest since 2002. The move puzzles traders who expect higher yields to hurt gold, which pays no interest. Peter Schiff calls rising yields 'the most bullish thing' for gold, arguing that bond investors losing money will sell bonds and buy gold or silver. Central banks keep buying: China added about 23 tonnes in September, its 23rd straight month, and global central banks bought a net 39 tonnes in August. Russia sold 56 tonnes this year. Delegates at the London gold conference see gold near $5,000 in a year. For now, gold trades well off January's record above $5,500, but the buying base remains solid.",
      "sources": [1, 4]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Strong jobs data keeps gold in tight range",
      "essay": "Initial jobless claims fell to 197,000 for the week ending October 3, below the 200,000 forecast. The four-week average was 198,000, and continuing claims came in at 1.716 million. Gold traded near the middle of its daily range after the data, last at $4,122.37 per ounce, up 0.27%. The strong labor market supports the case for the Federal Reserve to keep rates high, which pressures gold. But the metal held its ground, suggesting buyers are still present on dips.",
      "sources": [5]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Technical outlooks split on gold's next move",
      "essay": "Analysts are divided on gold's near-term path. Abhilash Koikkara of Nuvama sees MCX Gold range-bound with a negative bias, targeting Rs 1,45,000 and resistance at Rs 1,52,000. He notes lower highs and lower lows on the daily chart. Another forecast points to gold hanging around the $4,000 to $4,200 demand area, with rising interest rates and Middle East conflict in focus. European debt issues in France, Greece, and Italy could make gold more desirable. The mixed technical signals reflect the broader uncertainty.",
      "sources": [2, 3]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Infoedge/Naukri|https://www.saasri.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Mixed quarter. Billings grew 12.8% to ₹822.3 crore, but the stock fell 3% on Shiksha's 27.2% decline and a recruitment growth rate that trails pure-play SaaS peers. 99acres at 21.2% and matchmaking at 23.1% are the offsets. AI search is eating Shiksha's referral traffic, and the market is pricing that risk.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Info Edge Q2 billings up 12.8%, shares fall 3%",
      "essay": "Info Edge reported standalone billings of ₹822.3 crore for Q2 FY27, up 12.8% from ₹729 crore a year earlier. For H1 FY27, billings rose 13.5% to ₹1,559.3 crore. The stock still slipped 3% after the update.\n\nThe recruitment segment, which includes Naukri, grew 12.6% to ₹613.8 crore. Management said underlying growth was 14% to 15% once renewal timing differences are stripped out. That gap matters: renewal timing can flatter or depress reported growth from quarter to quarter. In Q1 FY27, recruitment billings grew 17.5% on a reported basis, but underlying growth was also around 15%.\n\nFor someone holding Info Edge, the read is that the core recruitment engine is growing in the mid-teens, not the 30%+ that pure-play SaaS firms report. The market is focused on that gap and on the Shiksha drag, not the headline beat.",
      "sources": [1, 2, 3, 5, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "99acres and matchmaking grow, Shiksha hit by AI search",
      "essay": "99acres billings rose 21.2% to ₹148.3 crore, accelerating from 16.5% growth in the previous quarter. SimilarWeb data cited by the company showed 99acres held 49% of category web time-share and 59% of app time-share in July and August 2026.\n\nMatchmaking also grew. Jeevansathi billings rose 18.4% to ₹39.7 crore, and Aisle jumped 40%. Combined, the matchmaking vertical grew 23.1%.\n\nShiksha was the weak spot. Billings fell 27.2% to ₹20.5 crore. Info Edge said AI-powered search tools now answer user queries directly, cutting referral traffic to the site. The company is adapting Shiksha's model to reduce search dependence, but expects growth to stay constrained in the interim. For H1 FY27, Shiksha billings were down 24.5% to ₹55.1 crore.\n\nThe pattern is clear: verticals that embed AI into the core user experience are growing, while one that depends on organic search traffic is shrinking.",
      "sources": [1, 2, 3, 5, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "AI-Rex and Talent Pulse see rising adoption",
      "essay": "Info Edge said adoption of its B2B AI products, AI-Rex and Talent Pulse, is increasing. Both recorded growth in new customer sign-ups and revenue contribution from new and existing customers.\n\nThese are early-stage products, and the company did not break out their billings. But they sit inside the recruitment segment, which grew 12.6% to ₹613.8 crore. The company is positioning AI-first tools as a way to lift recruitment growth without hurting unit economics.\n\nFor a holder, the signal is that Info Edge is trying to move beyond a legacy job-board model. The numbers are not yet large enough to change the overall growth rate, but they are the piece to watch for whether AI investment translates into higher net retention.",
      "sources": [1, 3, 5, 6]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Jio Platforms|https://www.linkedin.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Mixed news. The Jio IPO is moving forward, with reports of a Rs 37,700 crore issue and valuation talk of $110-140 billion. Airtel's tariff hike (Rs 50 on postpaid) gives Jio room to raise prices, but Jio says it will stay the most affordable. Jio's ARPU is Rs 215.6, still below Airtel's Rs 264. The IPO is the main event; the tariff stance is a near-term margin question.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Jio IPO on track; Akash Ambani calls it the biggest moment",
      "essay": "Jio Platforms' IPO is moving ahead, with the draft prospectus filed in June and final regulatory observations received on August 28. Akash Ambani, managing director of Jio Platforms and chairman of Reliance Jio, said the listing is the biggest moment for the company and carries a great responsibility to shareholders, including retail investors. He said it will share the value Jio has created with Indian citizens.\n\nThe offering is expected to be India's largest ever, with reports pointing to a Rs 37,700 crore issue opening around October 21. Valuation estimates range widely. One report says Jio is seeking Rs 11 lakh crore, while another says the offering could value the company between $110 billion and $140 billion and raise roughly $4 billion. The final price and investors will be known at the right time, Ambani said.",
      "sources": [1, 2, 3, 6]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Jio stays affordable as Airtel raises postpaid tariffs",
      "essay": "Bharti Airtel increased its postpaid tariffs by Rs 50 across the board, effective October 8, 2026, with plans that bundle one free international roaming trip a year. The hike is between 3% and 11% in percentage terms, and existing subscribers will be moved to the nearest higher plan within a month. Analysts expect telecom operators to raise tariffs by 12-15% over the next four to five months.\n\nJio Platforms managing director Akash Ambani said Jio will always remain the most affordable player while delivering quality service. He did not announce a tariff change. Jio's average revenue per user (ARPU) rose 3.3% year-on-year to Rs 215.6 in the second quarter of the current financial year. Airtel's ARPU grew 5.6% to Rs 264. The gap gives Jio room to raise prices later without losing its affordability position.",
      "sources": [2, 4, 5]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Jio builds 1,600-satellite constellation to take on Starlink",
      "essay": "At India Mobile Congress, Akash Ambani said Jio is building its own satellite stack, an end-to-end data system that supports 5G products and additional services. The constellation will have over 1,600 low-Earth orbit satellites and 23 ground stations. That would let Jio compete with SpaceX's Starlink, which operates more than 11,000 satellites.\n\nAmbani said the aim is for Jio to be a creator of technology, not only a buyer. The move fits the company's push to build an indigenous technology stack and offer affordable solutions for India's billion-plus users. For IPO investors, the satellite plan is a long-term bet on Jio as a full technology player, beyond mobile connectivity.",
      "sources": [1, 4, 5]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Gold|https://www.mitrade.com/au/in.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Gold's rebound above $4,200 is real but fragile. A weaker dollar and lower Treasury yields helped, while US-Iran de-escalation cut safe-haven demand. The Fed's next move decides direction, and officials are still talking about more hikes. Mixed signals, so no clear lean today.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Gold rebounds above $4,200 as dollar and yields ease",
      "essay": "Spot gold climbed back above $4,200 an ounce on Friday, 9 October 2026, recovering from a two-month low. The metal had fallen to around $4,130.80 intraday before buyers stepped in. Reuters reported spot gold rose more than 1% during the session.\n\nThe move came as the US dollar weakened and the 10-year Treasury yield declined for a second straight session. Gold pays no interest, so lower yields make it more attractive to hold. Lower oil prices also eased inflation concerns, which can pull bond yields down and support gold through that channel.\n\nPresident Trump said the US would not attack Iran before the November midterm elections, which reduced immediate geopolitical concerns. That cuts safe-haven demand for gold, but it also pushed oil lower, which may help gold by easing inflation pressure. The net effect is unclear.\n\nFor now, the question is whether gold can hold above $4,200 and push through $4,250. A sustained break higher could open the way to $4,300. If buyers fail, prices could slide back toward $4,100.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Fed rate path remains the biggest risk for gold",
      "essay": "Federal Reserve policy is still the main driver for gold in October. St. Louis Fed President Alberto Musalem said further rate increases may be needed to bring inflation back to target. Markets are weighing that against the chance that weaker data forces a pause.\n\nIf the Fed turns dovish, lower yields and a softer dollar would help gold. If inflation stays hot and the Fed tightens more, the opportunity cost of holding gold rises and the metal suffers. Gold is often called an inflation hedge, but higher rates can still push its price down.\n\nOn the data front, initial jobless claims came in at 197,000 for the week ending October 3, below the 200,000 forecast. The four-week average was 198,000. Continuing claims were 1.716 million. The strong labor market gives the Fed room to keep rates high, which is a headwind for gold.\n\nFor anyone holding gold, the Fed's next meeting and inflation readings matter more than any single day's price move.",
      "sources": [1, 5]
    },
    {
      "headline": "Schiff calls rising yields bullish for gold, sees default risk",
      "essay": "Peter Schiff told Kitco News that rising bond yields are \"the most bullish thing\" for gold, arguing that bond investors losing money will sell bonds and buy gold instead. He spoke as the 10-year Treasury yield touched 5.35%, its highest since 2002, before easing after a $39 billion auction.\n\nSchiff expects the 40-year bond bull market that ended in 2020-2021 to be followed by a bear market lasting maybe 20 years. He sees 9% mortgage rates by early next year and home prices falling 30% to 50%. He warns Washington could restructure its debt, paying lenders 50 cents on the dollar, which he calls a stealth default.\n\nCentral banks are still buying. China added about 23 tonnes in September, its 23rd straight month. Global central banks bought a net 39 tonnes in August. Russia sold 56 tonnes this year.\n\nSchiff's view is contrarian. Most traders sell gold when yields rise. He says that reflex is wrong and that gold will be the last safe haven standing.",
      "sources": [3]
    }
  ]
}

topic|HAL|https://www.tradingview.com/ne.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "Mixed signals for HAL. GE shipped 4 F404 engines in September and plans 30 per year, easing Tejas Mk1A delays. Honeywell will deliver all 16 HTT-40 engines by early 2027, ahead of schedule. But a retired Air Marshal says HAL has lost the IAF's faith, and analyst targets rose only 1.7% to INR 5672.8. Execution remains the swing factor.",
  "stories": [
    {
    "headline": "GE ships 4 more F404 engines, targets Tejas backlog by 2028",
    "essay": "GE Aerospace shipped four F404-IN20 engines to HAL in September 2026, bringing total deliveries to 14. GE shipped three engines in August after handing over six by the end of the 2025-26 financial year. The engines power the Tejas Mk1A under a $716 million contract signed in 2021 for 99 engines, enough for the first batch of 83 jets ordered by the IAF.\n\nHAL has built roughly 30 Mk1A airframes that are complete or nearly complete but waiting for engines. GE plans to deliver eight more engines by the end of 2026, then ramp to about 30 engines a year starting next year, aiming to clear the backlog by 2028. The IAF operates around 29 fighter squadrons against a sanctioned strength of 42.5, so the Tejas Mk1A is central to arresting the decline. The Mk1A adds an AESA radar, a modern electronic warfare suite, beyond-visual-range missiles and faster maintenance turnarounds.\n\nIf GE holds that schedule, HAL can fit its parked airframes, run flight tests and start handing over combat-ready jets in the coming months. That would directly address the production bottleneck that has held back the programme.",
    "sources": [5]
    },
    {
    "headline": "Honeywell to deliver all 16 HTT-40 engines by early 2027",
    "essay": "Honeywell Aerospace expects to deliver all 16 TPE331-12B engines for the HTT-40 trainer to HAL by the first half of 2027, ahead of the original mid-2029 schedule. Honeywell has already delivered four engines, including three in June 2026 and a fourth shortly after, and plans to hand over four more by the end of 2026.\n\nThe engines are part of a contract signed in July 2022 worth more than $100 million for 88 engines and kits, plus maintenance and support. Honeywell supplies the first 16 directly, then transfers technology so HAL can assemble the engines in India and expand repair and overhaul capabilities. The earlier delays came from supply chain problems after a fire destroyed a supplier's facility that made two engine components. Honeywell has added alternative sources and deployed its own employees to supplier sites.\n\nHAL delivered the first HTT-40 trainer, named Ankur, to the IAF in September. The IAF has ordered 70 trainers, though a think tank affiliated with the IAF puts the requirement at 181. Engine supply is only one part of the programme, and the broader aircraft work remains HAL's responsibility, so timely engines do not guarantee the trainer stays on schedule.",
    "sources": [2, 4]
    },
    {
    "headline": "Retired Air Marshal says HAL has lost IAF's faith",
    "essay": "Air Marshal Choudhury (R) said HAL has lost the IAF's faith, pointing to Tejas delays. The criticism lands as the IAF struggles with declining squadron strength. The service operates around 29 fighter squadrons against a sanctioned strength of 42.5, and the retirement of the MiG-21 has made the shortfall more acute.\n\nThe Tejas programme has run for decades. Full funding began in 1993, the first flight was in January 2001, and 25 years later the IAF still has not received enough Tejas to replace the phased-out MiG-21s. HAL has missed manufacturing timelines and blames the IAF for changing Air Staff Qualitative Requirements, the benchmarks for designing combat aircraft. The IAF blames HAL in turn. HAL's production lines cannot make the 36 to 40 combat aircraft a year the Air Chief wants, which points to expanding private production of Tejas.\n\nFor HAL, the reputational risk is real. The company is the main supplier of indigenous military aircraft, and its ability to deliver on time is now being questioned publicly by a former senior officer. That pressure could shape future orders and the pace of private competition.",
    "sources": [3, 6]
    }
  ]
}

topic|Tejas Networks|https://telecom.eco.text:
{
  "stance": "neutral",
  "view": "One story today, and it is about the sector, not Tejas. The Bharat 6G Alliance has grown past 90 members and reports a 30% cut in problem-solving cycles from early AI experiments. Tejas chairman NG Subramaniam is close to the effort, but no Tejas order, revenue or guidance is mentioned. Neutral until company-specific news lands.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "headline": "Bharat 6G Alliance expands past 90 members, claims AI gains",
      "essay": "The Bharat 6G Alliance has grown from 10 to 11 founding members to over 90 organisations, more than 30 of them deep-tech AI companies, chairman NG Subramaniam said at India Mobile Congress 2026. It runs seven working groups covering core technology, applications, spectrum, devices and emerging industries, plus international partnerships.\n\nSubramaniam, who is also non-executive chairman of Tejas Networks, said government R&D funding for the national 6G push exceeds $10 billion, alongside joint research with academia. Early experiments with foundational AI models cut problem-solving cycles by 30 per cent, letting engineering teams resolve complex network issues in half the usual time. Members are using those models to automate internal configurations and move away from rigid routing rules.\n\nOn monetisation, he said 6G will go past voice and broadband into intent-based, contextual and compute-driven services. Fee-based income could come from platforms around identity services, location intelligence, edge AI infrastructure and sector applications in farming, healthcare and education. For Tejas, the read is indirect: the alliance shapes standards and funding that its chairman helps steer, but no Tejas product, order or revenue figure appears in the article.",
      "sources": [1]
    }
  ]
}

